Oh geez.

00:22:36.366 --> 00:22:37.555
- Basically Facebook just make VTubers,

00:22:37.555 --> 00:22:40.405
'cause that will make the
interest just 10 times more.

00:22:40.405 --> 00:22:41.845
- Oh for sure.

00:22:41.845 --> 00:22:43.525
Yeah, well, interestingly enough,

00:22:43.525 --> 00:22:46.348
we did do a VR stream, myself
and all of the Mythrils,

00:22:46.348 --> 00:22:48.263
we did a, for our anniversary,

00:22:48.263 --> 00:22:50.544
we all got into VR chat
and we actually all bought

00:22:50.544 --> 00:22:54.818
the suits to track our
movements and stuff like that.

00:22:54.818 --> 00:22:55.821
- Oh wow.

00:22:55.821 --> 00:22:57.549
- We went as small versions of ourselves.

00:22:57.549 --> 00:22:59.236
- I saw this, yeah, I did see it, yeah.

00:22:59.236 --> 00:23:02.417
- That was so fun and it just made me want

00:23:02.417 --> 00:23:03.642
to meet with the others even more,

00:23:03.642 --> 00:23:06.622
so that we can really,
fully debut, someday in 3D.

00:23:06.622 --> 00:23:09.446
- Is it weird wearing
a full tracking suit?

00:23:09.446 --> 00:23:11.024
- What is the full tracking suit?

00:23:11.024 --> 00:23:13.561
Is it the one like the mo-cap?

00:23:13.561 --> 00:23:14.902
Is it the actual mo-cap suits?

00:23:14.902 --> 00:23:17.567
Or is it the one where you, it's on the-

00:23:17.567 --> 00:23:20.591
- I think it's two on the
hands, two on the feet,

00:23:20.591 --> 00:23:22.222
and then one on your waist.

00:23:22.222 --> 00:23:23.949
- Yeah, you track your waste movements.

00:23:23.949 --> 00:23:25.290
I had a little too much
fun with that though

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:26.306
with all the tracking.

00:23:26.306 --> 00:23:27.139
(Garnt and Joey laughing)

00:23:27.139 --> 00:23:28.796
I was moving around,
maybe a little too much,

00:23:28.796 --> 00:23:30.578
probably could have dialed it back a bit,

00:23:30.578 --> 00:23:32.254
but I was so excited to be in a 3D space,

00:23:32.254 --> 00:23:34.490
able to move with my friends.

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:35.709
- Yeah, VR is definitely
like that the first time.

00:23:35.709 --> 00:23:37.004
You're like, "Holy shit."

00:23:37.004 --> 00:23:38.670
And then you've realized
that you're sweating a lot

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:41.433
and then you've burned 10,000 calories

00:23:41.433 --> 00:23:42.536
because you were having so much fun.

00:23:42.536 --> 00:23:43.395
(Garnt laughs)

00:23:43.395 --> 00:23:45.010
Have you played this
game called Gorilla Tag?

00:23:45.010 --> 00:23:46.168
(Connor chuckles)

00:23:46.168 --> 00:23:47.001
- [Garnt] No.
- [Mori] What?

00:23:47.001 --> 00:23:49.708
(Garnt chuckles)

00:23:49.708 --> 00:23:51.028
- On the topic of VR,

00:23:51.028 --> 00:23:53.849
so Connor had his house
warming party the other day,

00:23:53.849 --> 00:23:56.963
and Chris was there
and seeing Chris try VR

00:23:56.963 --> 00:24:00.682
for the first time was a
fucking site to behold.

00:24:00.682 --> 00:24:02.280
- We gave Chris a few drinks.

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:03.288
(Garnt laughs)

00:24:03.288 --> 00:24:04.190
And I was like-
- A few.

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:08.013
- I was like, Chris, you
should try playing Superhot.

00:24:08.013 --> 00:24:10.468
(Joey laughs)

00:24:10.468 --> 00:24:12.123
- [Mori] Superhot, oh no.

00:24:12.123 --> 00:24:12.964
- And Chris was like-

00:24:12.964 --> 00:24:13.797
- I'm so upset I missed out on that.

00:24:13.797 --> 00:24:16.368
- Dude, he was rolling on the
floor, doing jumps and shit,

00:24:16.368 --> 00:24:19.170
like James Bond in my bedroom.
(Garnt laughs)

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:21.527
Smacking the walls, nearly
broke all my JoJo posters.

00:24:21.527 --> 00:24:22.360
- First thing I see,

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:24.540
I walk in and Chris is
fucking punching something.

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:27.251
He punches a JoJo poster right there.

00:24:27.251 --> 00:24:28.084
- He nearly broke it.

00:24:28.084 --> 00:24:29.979
(Mori laughs)
Just full on punching in VR.

00:24:29.979 --> 00:24:32.133
I'm like, Chris, it's okay, it's okay.

00:24:32.133 --> 00:24:32.966
(Joey and Garnt laughs)

00:24:32.966 --> 00:24:34.629
Guy nearly destroyed my apartment.

00:24:34.629 --> 00:24:36.261
He was like, what is this?

00:24:36.261 --> 00:24:37.850
It's shit, it's shit, he
put it on, he loved it.

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:38.902
(Garnt laughs)

00:24:38.902 --> 00:24:39.749
It's classic Chris.

00:24:39.749 --> 00:24:40.582
- Well, that's VR, right.

00:24:40.582 --> 00:24:41.982
I've tried to talk so many people into VR

00:24:41.982 --> 00:24:43.364
and they're just like,
nah, I don't get it,

00:24:43.364 --> 00:24:44.588
it's probably not for me.

00:24:44.588 --> 00:24:45.569
It's definitely one of those things

00:24:45.569 --> 00:24:46.805
that you just have to try.

00:24:46.805 --> 00:24:49.307
And it's like, once you try
it, then you fully understand.

00:24:49.307 --> 00:24:52.308
You could give a fucking
dissertation on why VR is awesome,

00:24:52.308 --> 00:24:53.551
and you probably couldn't get-

00:24:53.551 --> 00:24:55.750
- I mean, the thing about VR
is that I can only play VR

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:57.109
for a certain amount of time

00:24:57.109 --> 00:24:59.750
before it just feels less casual.

00:24:59.750 --> 00:25:00.969
I don't know.

00:25:00.969 --> 00:25:02.126
I sweat a lot in VR.

00:25:02.126 --> 00:25:03.827
- You gotta play Gorilla Tag,

00:25:03.827 --> 00:25:04.968
that's where the real-

00:25:04.968 --> 00:25:05.801
- What's Gorilla Tag?

00:25:05.801 --> 00:25:06.634
- Yeah, how do you play it?

00:25:06.634 --> 00:25:07.467
- It's exactly what it sounds like.

00:25:07.467 --> 00:25:08.777
You're gorillas and you tag each other.

00:25:08.777 --> 00:25:09.828
(Joey laughs)
- [Mori] Oh boy.

00:25:09.828 --> 00:25:10.661
- I did it on stream, right,

00:25:10.661 --> 00:25:13.490
I thought it would just be chasing people.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:15.371
It turns out it's just 10 year olds,

00:25:15.371 --> 00:25:17.194
just playing as gorillas.

00:25:17.194 --> 00:25:18.688
- [Garnt] What do you mean?

00:25:18.688 --> 00:25:19.707
- It's all 10 year olds.

00:25:19.707 --> 00:25:22.209
I joined a lobby and 10
year olds were swearing

00:25:22.209 --> 00:25:23.042
and saying horrible things.

00:25:23.042 --> 00:25:23.945
- [Mori] Ah, that sounds about right.

00:25:23.945 --> 00:25:24.778
- And I was like-

00:25:24.778 --> 00:25:25.611
- So it's just XBox live all over again.

00:25:25.611 --> 00:25:26.444
(Mori laughs)

00:25:26.444 --> 00:25:28.185
- If you want Xbox, go and
play Gorilla Tag, right.

00:25:28.185 --> 00:25:29.207
Because what happens is, the way you-

00:25:29.207 --> 00:25:31.084
- Connor gets to relive his childhood,

00:25:31.084 --> 00:25:32.127
that's why he loved it so much.

00:25:32.127 --> 00:25:33.816
(all laughing)

00:25:33.816 --> 00:25:34.649
- No, no.

00:25:36.619 --> 00:25:37.577
I did not like this.

00:25:37.577 --> 00:25:39.354
I realized the power you have as a kid

00:25:39.354 --> 00:25:41.437
is unobtainable as an adult.

00:25:41.437 --> 00:25:42.802
Those kids could say and do anything.

00:25:42.802 --> 00:25:46.294
I can't say that shit back
to them, it's so unfair.

00:25:46.294 --> 00:25:48.264
The kids can say whatever
they want and it's like-

00:25:48.264 --> 00:25:50.145
- What do you mean it's unfair?

00:25:50.145 --> 00:25:51.985
- I want to be able to call
that kid a little shit.

00:25:51.985 --> 00:25:53.889
- Well, why can't you call
that kid a little shit?

00:25:53.889 --> 00:25:55.094
- I'm on stream, I can't say
it, off stream I'll say it.

00:25:55.094 --> 00:25:56.403
(Connor and Joey laughs)

00:25:56.403 --> 00:25:57.236
Come here, you little shit.

00:25:57.236 --> 00:25:58.315
- [Mori] Chat will do it for
you, chat will do it for you.

00:25:58.315 --> 00:26:00.008
- Get over here.
- [Mori] On your behalf.

00:26:00.008 --> 00:26:02.655
- Yeah, yeah, please come in and just-

00:26:02.655 --> 00:26:03.694
- What were they saying to you?

00:26:03.694 --> 00:26:08.071
- They were saying words that
you cannot say on stream.

00:26:08.071 --> 00:26:08.904
Some-

00:26:08.904 --> 00:26:09.737
- Were you streaming?

00:26:09.737 --> 00:26:10.570
- And I was streaming.

00:26:10.570 --> 00:26:12.892
There's a clip of me, I go
in a world, I joined it,

00:26:12.892 --> 00:26:17.015
and one of the kids was saying
the word that you cannot say.

00:26:17.015 --> 00:26:18.031
- Yeah.

00:26:18.031 --> 00:26:20.119
- And I was like, all
right then, can I go away?

00:26:20.119 --> 00:26:22.599
And then just left the
world 'cause he was,

00:26:22.599 --> 00:26:24.135
this 10 year old kid was screaming it,

00:26:24.135 --> 00:26:25.560
but it's really fun, right?

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:26.541
So you do this, you have the controllers,

00:26:26.541 --> 00:26:27.858
and they act as your-

00:26:27.858 --> 00:26:29.037
- It sounds like a bunch of apes in there.

00:26:29.037 --> 00:26:30.738
(Garnt and Mori laughs)

00:26:30.738 --> 00:26:33.197
- So you have two of the
VR controllers, right?

00:26:33.197 --> 00:26:34.140
- Yeah.

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:35.335
- And those are your legs and your arms,

00:26:35.335 --> 00:26:39.074
so when you want to move you
to have jump with the legs.

00:26:39.074 --> 00:26:40.194
- Oh okay, yeah.

00:26:40.194 --> 00:26:41.027
- To push off them.

00:26:41.027 --> 00:26:43.313
And people are insane,
these kids are insane.

00:26:43.313 --> 00:26:45.384
They're like flying across
the map and I can barely move.

00:26:45.384 --> 00:26:46.819
I really felt like a boomer.

00:26:46.819 --> 00:26:47.857
(Garnt, Mori and Joey laughs)

00:26:47.857 --> 00:26:49.198
You should play Gorilla Tag.

00:26:49.198 --> 00:26:50.115
- Oh, sure.

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:52.066
- How long do you play VR for though?

00:26:52.066 --> 00:26:53.538
How long did you stream for?

00:26:53.538 --> 00:26:55.165
Three hours, four hours, maybe.

00:26:55.165 --> 00:26:55.998
- Three hours?

00:26:55.998 --> 00:26:56.831
Did you not get sweaty?

00:26:56.831 --> 00:26:57.664
- Extremely so.

00:26:57.664 --> 00:26:59.326
- Okay, that's what puts me off VR.

00:26:59.326 --> 00:27:01.898
It's like I stream, I play
for now, I get really sweaty,

00:27:01.898 --> 00:27:05.166
and then you don't take the VR set off,

00:27:05.166 --> 00:27:07.853
you fucking peel it off
at that point, right?

00:27:07.853 --> 00:27:08.954
It just doesn't feel good.

00:27:08.954 --> 00:27:11.195
- At least the Index has
the removable face thing.

00:27:11.195 --> 00:27:12.207
- [Garnt] Yeah.

00:27:12.207 --> 00:27:14.837
- So you can clean it, so I
didn't feel too disgusted by it.

00:27:14.837 --> 00:27:18.476
- So you're just cycling
through the foam parts.

00:27:18.476 --> 00:27:20.334
- I'm cleaning it a lot.

00:27:20.334 --> 00:27:21.379
- [Garnt] Okay.

00:27:21.379 --> 00:27:22.592
- Wait, which headset are you using?

00:27:22.592 --> 00:27:25.651
- I'm using the Valve Index,
yeah, it's super good.

00:27:25.651 --> 00:27:27.334
- I hate wires though.

00:27:27.334 --> 00:27:28.665
- [Garnt] I hate that as well.

00:27:28.665 --> 00:27:30.548
- The wires are the only
thing that take you out of it,

00:27:30.548 --> 00:27:31.381
I feel like.

00:27:31.381 --> 00:27:33.952
- Yeah, I feel like, once
it gets to wireless headsets

00:27:33.952 --> 00:27:36.616
that are high quality, then I would get

00:27:36.616 --> 00:27:38.132
more invested into VR.

00:27:38.132 --> 00:27:39.292
- Oh, Oculus 2, right?

00:27:39.292 --> 00:27:40.273
It's the wireless one, right?

00:27:40.273 --> 00:27:41.654
- Yeah, I think the specs aren't as good

00:27:41.654 --> 00:27:44.890
as the Index though, but I mean,

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:46.124
it's pretty good from
what I've heard though.

00:27:46.124 --> 00:27:47.819
- I haven't streamed on my Oculus 2 yet,

00:27:47.819 --> 00:27:50.332
but I bought it just because
I heard that there's no wires.

00:27:50.332 --> 00:27:52.165
I'm like, good, that's great.

00:27:52.165 --> 00:27:53.935
- The Index is super easy to use.

00:27:53.935 --> 00:27:56.441
- Was that the first
time you had tried VR,

00:27:56.441 --> 00:27:58.797
or had you tried VR before your stream?

00:27:58.797 --> 00:28:02.181
- Actually, to be honest,
I kind of wasn't interested

00:28:02.181 --> 00:28:05.461
at all in it, but Amelia's
like the tech wizard

00:28:05.461 --> 00:28:08.021
in Myth and she was just kinda like,

00:28:08.021 --> 00:28:11.293
"Hey, what if we did a big VR
collab for our anniversary?"

00:28:11.293 --> 00:28:13.296
And all of us were like, okay, but how?

00:28:13.296 --> 00:28:14.680
And she's like, no, it's super easy.

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:16.735
And she told us everything
we needed to buy.

00:28:16.735 --> 00:28:18.993
- That sounds like a technical nightmare.

00:28:18.993 --> 00:28:19.826
- [Connor] Gamer god.

00:28:19.826 --> 00:28:21.176
- My God. Oh my God.

00:28:21.176 --> 00:28:24.792
Imagine trying to schedule
that and sort that shit out.

00:28:24.792 --> 00:28:26.755
I go through enough technical issues,

00:28:26.755 --> 00:28:29.257
just streaming on my PC.
- [Joey] On my own.

00:28:29.257 --> 00:28:30.256
- On my own.

00:28:30.256 --> 00:28:33.420
Let alone doing a cross
continental VR stream.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:34.454
- [Joey] Oh my God.

00:28:34.454 --> 00:28:35.456
- With motion capture.

00:28:35.456 --> 00:28:36.355
- She worked really hard.

00:28:36.355 --> 00:28:39.254
- I'm curious, is there
anything with being

00:28:39.254 --> 00:28:40.897
a VTuber over the past
year that you've learned

00:28:40.897 --> 00:28:44.317
is annoyingly difficult that other people

00:28:44.317 --> 00:28:45.743
would just never consider?

00:28:45.743 --> 00:28:49.076
- Oh, let me think, this is a tough one.

00:28:50.161 --> 00:28:50.994
One sec.

00:28:52.038 --> 00:28:52.871
- [Connor] No worries, no worries.

00:28:52.871 --> 00:28:53.782
- You gotta think 'cause
there are a lot of things

00:28:53.782 --> 00:28:54.623
that looks fun.

00:28:54.623 --> 00:28:56.569
- 'Cause I feel like I don't
appreciate how difficult

00:28:56.569 --> 00:28:57.402
it can be sometimes.

00:28:57.402 --> 00:28:58.385
- [Garnt] Yeah.

00:28:58.385 --> 00:28:59.848
- I just look at them and I just think,

00:28:59.848 --> 00:29:01.607
"Oh wow, you guys don't have
to worry about how you look,

00:29:01.607 --> 00:29:05.706
you can just change the model,
it's great, I'm so jealous."

00:29:05.706 --> 00:29:07.511
- I mean, there's a lot of stuff,

00:29:07.511 --> 00:29:09.107
behind the scenes that we have to do,

00:29:09.107 --> 00:29:10.146
is the thing.

00:29:10.146 --> 00:29:10.979
A lot of people,

00:29:10.979 --> 00:29:14.103
they see us for maybe two, three hours,

00:29:14.103 --> 00:29:16.247
if you stream for a short amount of time,

00:29:16.247 --> 00:29:18.338
if you do like an endurance
stream, eight hours, sure.

00:29:18.338 --> 00:29:22.471
But here's the thing though,
when we press go live,

00:29:22.471 --> 00:29:25.054
and after we're done, stop the stream,

00:29:25.054 --> 00:29:27.591
immediately what I do is
I go sit on the couch,

00:29:27.591 --> 00:29:29.103
and I start making thumbnails.

00:29:29.103 --> 00:29:30.142
- [Connor] God.

00:29:30.142 --> 00:29:31.584
- I make a bunch of
thumbnails for the week,

00:29:31.584 --> 00:29:32.847
then I have to make the schedule,

00:29:32.847 --> 00:29:33.944
which last time, took me
about an hour and a half.

00:29:33.944 --> 00:29:36.134
- So do you make the thumbnails yourself?

00:29:36.134 --> 00:29:36.977
- [Mori] Yes.

00:29:36.977 --> 00:29:37.810
- Oh my God.

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:38.817
- I think most VTubers do, right?

00:29:38.817 --> 00:29:39.650
- What?

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:40.483
- [Garnt] Why?

00:29:40.483 --> 00:29:42.145
(Garnt laughs)

00:29:42.145 --> 00:29:43.807
- That's the one thing I'm so impressed

00:29:43.807 --> 00:29:45.136
with VTubers is that they always

00:29:45.136 --> 00:29:46.736
have unique thumbnails for everything.

00:29:46.736 --> 00:29:48.977
And I'm like, I didn't
realize how grateful I am

00:29:48.977 --> 00:29:50.132
that Twitch, you don't have to do that.

00:29:50.132 --> 00:29:51.809
- Yeah, how did you learn how to do that?

00:29:51.809 --> 00:29:54.787
Because like not to be
like a dumb question,

00:29:54.787 --> 00:29:57.371
but thumbnails are like a very fine art

00:29:57.371 --> 00:30:01.254
that a lot of YouTubers
learn as they go along.

00:30:01.254 --> 00:30:03.309
I mean, I didn't know how
to make good thumbnails

00:30:03.309 --> 00:30:05.706
until a year or two after I
started doing it properly.

00:30:05.706 --> 00:30:06.797
- Well, there's people on the platform

00:30:06.797 --> 00:30:07.900
that have been on for like 10 years

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:09.004
that still don't know
how to make those well.

00:30:09.004 --> 00:30:10.020
- Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:30:10.020 --> 00:30:11.338
- I mean, it's something

00:30:11.338 --> 00:30:13.382
that you just kind of learn along the way,

00:30:13.382 --> 00:30:15.059
because for me, I had no idea.

00:30:15.059 --> 00:30:16.760
My thumbnail game was real weak before,

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:18.141
but I had to get good at it.

00:30:18.141 --> 00:30:21.038
And I started kind of seeing
what others did as well,

00:30:21.038 --> 00:30:23.401
and not completely taking
everything that everyone did,

00:30:23.401 --> 00:30:25.038
but little things here
and there that I had,

00:30:25.038 --> 00:30:26.477
I noticed and be like, ah,

00:30:26.477 --> 00:30:28.478
I should do a stroke layer
on this text like this,

00:30:28.478 --> 00:30:30.963
and it'll look a lot better
and a drop shadow here,

00:30:30.963 --> 00:30:32.816
and that kind of stuff.

00:30:32.816 --> 00:30:34.899
And it's all just fine little things,

00:30:34.899 --> 00:30:36.759
you'd never really think super hard about,

00:30:36.759 --> 00:30:38.063
but they do make a difference.

00:30:38.063 --> 00:30:39.125
- Yeah, exactly.

00:30:39.125 --> 00:30:42.503
How long do you guys
spend on your thumbnails?

00:30:42.503 --> 00:30:44.724
- I try to do it as little as possible.

00:30:44.724 --> 00:30:45.961
I try and outsource it as much as I can.

00:30:45.961 --> 00:30:46.794
(Joey laughs)

00:30:46.794 --> 00:30:48.032
- See this guy's working smart.

00:30:48.032 --> 00:30:50.993
I think I spend maybe
like, max, 30 minutes.

00:30:50.993 --> 00:30:53.553
I'm quite short when it
comes to thumbnails, yeah.

00:30:53.553 --> 00:30:55.678
- I feel like if you have the capacity

00:30:55.678 --> 00:30:57.355
to pay someone else to do something,

00:30:57.355 --> 00:30:59.064
you should pay someone
else to do something.

00:30:59.064 --> 00:31:00.170
(Joey snickers)
(Mori laughs)

00:31:00.170 --> 00:31:01.192
- [Garnt] No but like-

00:31:01.192 --> 00:31:03.793
- It's work it's business, I
want more time to do my stuff.

00:31:03.793 --> 00:31:04.813
- Not exactly but-

00:31:04.813 --> 00:31:05.730
- For some people they value

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:07.113
the creative aspect.
- Some privilege, I know.

00:31:07.113 --> 00:31:07.946
Shut up, shut up.

00:31:07.946 --> 00:31:08.779
(Chad laughs)

00:31:08.779 --> 00:31:09.662
See Chad laughing at me.

00:31:09.662 --> 00:31:10.719
(Mori and Garnt laughs)

00:31:10.719 --> 00:31:13.981
- Yeah, I mean, the thing
is, for example, for VTubers,

00:31:13.981 --> 00:31:16.141
there are so many of them
that want to be in control

00:31:16.141 --> 00:31:19.705
of their own thumbnails, that
it becomes a creativity thing.

00:31:19.705 --> 00:31:21.464
- I feel like thumbnails are
important because with you,

00:31:21.464 --> 00:31:23.941
you've got an artist doing
a lot of your thumbnails.

00:31:23.941 --> 00:31:26.346
And aside from that, it's
like taking the perfect shot

00:31:26.346 --> 00:31:28.279
on scene or something.

00:31:28.279 --> 00:31:30.839
Whereas my thumbnails can take up to like,

00:31:30.839 --> 00:31:33.741
some thumbnails, I can
outsource, some I can't

00:31:33.741 --> 00:31:35.727
because even I don't
know how to market some

00:31:35.727 --> 00:31:36.775
of my videos, right?

00:31:36.775 --> 00:31:39.935
I don't know, for me, when
you upload twice a week,

00:31:39.935 --> 00:31:42.071
or recently once a month,

00:31:42.071 --> 00:31:43.776
you need to make that
fucking thumbnail count, man.

00:31:43.776 --> 00:31:45.825
- That's why twice a
week can double my views.

00:31:45.825 --> 00:31:48.965
- No, sorry, twice a month, twice a month.

00:31:48.965 --> 00:31:50.004
Once every two weeks.

00:31:50.004 --> 00:31:51.293
You need to make those thumbnails count,

00:31:51.293 --> 00:31:52.773
with Trash Taste it's just easy.

00:31:52.773 --> 00:31:55.229
If one thumbnail doesn't
hit, you got next week.

00:31:55.229 --> 00:31:57.307
With me, I'm just like, "If
this thumbnail doesn't hit,

00:31:57.307 --> 00:31:58.909
I gotta wait a fucking month, man."

00:31:58.909 --> 00:31:59.742
(Mori laughs)

00:31:59.742 --> 00:32:01.075
That shit hurts.

00:32:02.845 --> 00:32:05.803
- Have you thought about,
perhaps, uploading more often.

00:32:05.803 --> 00:32:06.658
(Mori and Garnt laughs)

00:32:06.658 --> 00:32:07.750
- Well, that means making content

00:32:07.750 --> 00:32:09.454
that's faster to make.
- The pressure.

00:32:09.454 --> 00:32:10.945
- Yeah, yeah.

00:32:10.945 --> 00:32:11.778
- There's less pressure

00:32:11.778 --> 00:32:13.582
on making that perfect
thumbnail, all right.

00:32:13.582 --> 00:32:15.777
- Joey, I am making as much
content as I possibly can,

00:32:15.777 --> 00:32:17.535
Joey, please, okay.

00:32:17.535 --> 00:32:19.997
- I mean, I think another
thing that people also

00:32:19.997 --> 00:32:23.692
don't realize is, one big
factor, is just simply

00:32:23.692 --> 00:32:27.275
the energy it takes to
be you all the time.

00:32:29.127 --> 00:32:31.043
When I'm putting up a
schedule for a new stream,

00:32:31.043 --> 00:32:33.235
I've got to put in a
description that I think,

00:32:33.235 --> 00:32:36.272
kind of, either matches
or is this too much?

00:32:36.272 --> 00:32:37.956
Am I saying too many
things in the description?

00:32:37.956 --> 00:32:39.465
You overthink a lot in this job.

00:32:39.465 --> 00:32:42.008
Am I going to put out a tweet
that says a lot of things?

00:32:42.008 --> 00:32:44.289
Or one sentence, a couple words?

00:32:44.289 --> 00:32:48.782
You have to always think
about this kind of stuff.

00:32:48.782 --> 00:32:50.660
And scheduling meetings as
well, that's another big thing.

00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:52.643
- It's interesting that you
mentioned about description

00:32:52.643 --> 00:32:56.398
and tags, I guess and
the tweet and everything,

00:32:56.398 --> 00:32:57.975
'cause I think over the six years,

00:32:57.975 --> 00:32:59.755
what I've learned is
that I just don't care

00:32:59.755 --> 00:33:00.817
about any of that now.

00:33:00.817 --> 00:33:02.094
(Mori and Joey chuckles)

00:33:02.094 --> 00:33:03.341
Calli, do you care about
your description anymore?

00:33:03.341 --> 00:33:04.174
- No.

00:33:04.174 --> 00:33:05.380
- I used to care, right.

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:07.926
And then I found out
that all the big channels

00:33:07.926 --> 00:33:08.965
just don't do any of that.

00:33:08.965 --> 00:33:10.565
They don't put anything in the tags.

00:33:10.565 --> 00:33:12.741
- I think my experience,
there's two things that matters,

00:33:12.741 --> 00:33:13.766
title, thumbnail.

00:33:13.766 --> 00:33:14.599
- [Connor] It's literally that.

00:33:14.599 --> 00:33:15.884
- That's literally the
only real thing you need

00:33:15.884 --> 00:33:18.699
to worry about, tags and stuff
and descriptions and stuff,

00:33:18.699 --> 00:33:20.522
it doesn't help as much anymore.

00:33:20.522 --> 00:33:22.258
And I know this now

00:33:22.258 --> 00:33:24.509
because, have you guys gone
through this issue recently

00:33:24.509 --> 00:33:25.629
with YouTube, right?

00:33:25.629 --> 00:33:29.572
Where you search for a for
a certain video, right?

00:33:29.572 --> 00:33:32.369
And then the first three
searches are pertaining

00:33:32.369 --> 00:33:33.389
to that search.

00:33:33.389 --> 00:33:35.196
And then for some reason
after about five searches,

00:33:35.196 --> 00:33:38.360
it just goes back to your
recommendations just nowadays.

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:39.644
I'm just like, what is the point

00:33:39.644 --> 00:33:41.025
of the search function anymore?

00:33:41.025 --> 00:33:43.410
If the first five videos,
aren't what I'm searching for,

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:44.448
then I'm just fucked.

00:33:44.448 --> 00:33:45.728
- Or it's like, "The
viewers of your channel

00:33:45.728 --> 00:33:47.142
also viewed this."

00:33:47.142 --> 00:33:48.459
It's like, I don't care.
(Mori laughs)

00:33:48.459 --> 00:33:49.533
That's not what I searched for.

00:33:49.533 --> 00:33:51.878
- That's literally not
what I searched for.

00:33:51.878 --> 00:33:53.962
It's gone to the point where,

00:33:53.962 --> 00:33:55.939
back when YouTube started,
it's just like, okay,

00:33:55.939 --> 00:33:58.403
I subscribe to the
channels I want to watch,

00:33:58.403 --> 00:33:59.842
YouTube's like, "No, no, no, no,

00:33:59.842 --> 00:34:00.675
you don't want to watch these channels."

00:34:00.675 --> 00:34:01.626
"You want to watch these channels."

00:34:01.626 --> 00:34:04.312
And I'm like, okay, maybe I
won't get recommendations.

00:34:04.312 --> 00:34:07.232
Now, it's just like, I want to
search for a specific video,

00:34:07.232 --> 00:34:08.654
and YouTube is like, "No, no, no, no, no."

00:34:08.654 --> 00:34:09.693
- [Joey] "No, you don't."

00:34:09.693 --> 00:34:10.526
- "You don't want to search for that."

00:34:10.526 --> 00:34:11.359
- Yes, because of the algorithm.

00:34:11.359 --> 00:34:12.192
- "You want to watch these videos."

00:34:12.192 --> 00:34:14.295
- The ones that annoy me the
most is after the fifth search.

00:34:14.295 --> 00:34:15.968
It's like, "Here's a
new video from someone

00:34:15.968 --> 00:34:17.070
you're subscribed to."

00:34:17.070 --> 00:34:18.475
I'm like, "What is the
point of the subscriptions?"

00:34:18.475 --> 00:34:19.392
(Garnt and Mori laughs)

00:34:19.392 --> 00:34:22.150
I can check that, there was
an entire page for that.

00:34:22.150 --> 00:34:24.390
- What is the point of pages now?

00:34:24.390 --> 00:34:25.453
'Cause all pages are just gonna become

00:34:25.453 --> 00:34:26.936
the same page eventually, right.

00:34:26.936 --> 00:34:29.208
- Exactly, so annoying.

00:34:29.208 --> 00:34:30.752
- What is the point of the search bar?

00:34:30.752 --> 00:34:32.714
So yeah, to me descriptions
and tags don't mean

00:34:32.714 --> 00:34:34.073
as much anymore.

00:34:34.073 --> 00:34:37.776
It's all about title,
thumbnail, viewer retention,

00:34:37.776 --> 00:34:39.851
because it's all recommendations,

00:34:39.851 --> 00:34:42.670
we all bow down to the almighty algorithm.

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:46.408
And that's unfortunately what you gotta do

00:34:46.408 --> 00:34:47.728
to be a YouTuber nowadays.

00:34:47.728 --> 00:34:48.605
- [Joey] Yeah, pretty much.

00:34:48.605 --> 00:34:49.839
- And I mean, I guess it does
make sense that it should be,

00:34:49.839 --> 00:34:51.703
what you say and what you create,

00:34:51.703 --> 00:34:54.159
and not necessarily what you're typing

00:34:54.159 --> 00:34:55.655
into the description, right?

00:34:55.655 --> 00:34:57.659
I mean, every time that
I see, for example,

00:34:57.659 --> 00:34:59.903
some of my gem mates, they
just throw up a schedule,

00:34:59.903 --> 00:35:00.736
nothing in the descriptions and I'm like,

00:35:00.736 --> 00:35:02.121
"Wow, what a bunch of Chads."

00:35:02.121 --> 00:35:04.378
(Garnt laughs)

00:35:04.378 --> 00:35:06.503
It doesn't matter but
I mean, I don't know.

00:35:06.503 --> 00:35:07.727
I guess I'm still,
there's still some things

00:35:07.727 --> 00:35:08.929
I'm figuring out, day by day.

00:35:08.929 --> 00:35:11.866
I've gotten a lot better
than I was in the past.

00:35:11.866 --> 00:35:13.585
And I feel good about that, so

00:35:13.585 --> 00:35:16.126
day by day though, you can
always learn new stuff,

00:35:16.126 --> 00:35:17.166
especially in this job.

00:35:17.166 --> 00:35:19.419
- I feel you've done a
shit load in just a year.

00:35:19.419 --> 00:35:20.252
(Mori laughs)

00:35:20.252 --> 00:35:22.664
- Considering what you've
learnt in this year

00:35:22.664 --> 00:35:24.725
and what you've done in this
year, it's actually insane,

00:35:24.725 --> 00:35:26.867
compared to, I look back
on some of the years

00:35:26.867 --> 00:35:28.058
I've had on YouTube and I'm just like,

00:35:28.058 --> 00:35:29.299
"I'm doing fucking nothing."

00:35:29.299 --> 00:35:31.577
- How do you mainly, would
you say, you learn things

00:35:31.577 --> 00:35:33.444
on streaming and YouTube?

00:35:33.444 --> 00:35:36.358
Do you do learn from other gem mates,

00:35:36.358 --> 00:35:38.784
or do you learn from outside of hololive,

00:35:38.784 --> 00:35:39.881
or do you learn all on yourself?

00:35:39.881 --> 00:35:43.155
I'm curious where you kind
of pick up this stuff.

00:35:43.155 --> 00:35:45.495
- A lot of times it's
like a feeling that I get,

00:35:45.495 --> 00:35:46.836
like after I'm done with a stream,

00:35:46.836 --> 00:35:49.636
I can feel whether it was bad or good.

00:35:49.636 --> 00:35:51.197
- [Connor] Oh no, I agree, I agree.

00:35:51.197 --> 00:35:52.654
- I can feel what I did wrong.

00:35:52.654 --> 00:35:53.511
This technically wasn't good,

00:35:53.511 --> 00:35:55.673
these people were angry
about this, et cetera.

00:35:55.673 --> 00:35:56.892
And it's all in my mind.

00:35:56.892 --> 00:35:58.823
And I'm very receptive to criticism,

00:35:58.823 --> 00:36:01.126
but something that I also learned is

00:36:01.126 --> 00:36:03.707
that not every criticism that you get

00:36:03.707 --> 00:36:06.466
that is framed politely
is necessarily correct.

00:36:06.466 --> 00:36:07.469
- [Connor] True.

00:36:07.469 --> 00:36:08.302
- That's a lie that we tell ourselves,

00:36:08.302 --> 00:36:09.791
so that we don't come
off as butthurt babies.

00:36:09.791 --> 00:36:11.306
But the truth of the matter is,

00:36:11.306 --> 00:36:13.767
you don't need to 100%

00:36:13.767 --> 00:36:16.304
take every single critique that you get

00:36:16.304 --> 00:36:17.804
from every anonymous
person on the internet

00:36:17.804 --> 00:36:19.324
even if it's framed nicely,

00:36:19.324 --> 00:36:21.987
you need to be able to
discern what is actually going

00:36:21.987 --> 00:36:23.647
to help you and what isn't.

00:36:23.647 --> 00:36:25.569
But one thing that you
shouldn't do is lash out.

00:36:25.569 --> 00:36:27.770
I would say that's a given.

00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:32.459
- Never feed the trolls
'cause it never ends up well.

00:36:32.459 --> 00:36:33.498
- There are people that get excited

00:36:33.498 --> 00:36:36.418
when I talk about them and
the bad things they say.

00:36:36.418 --> 00:36:38.838
And the thing that you do
is you don't give it to them

00:36:38.838 --> 00:36:39.854
and you just ignore.

00:36:39.854 --> 00:36:42.374
- I mean, yeah, it's rule number
one, don't feed the trolls.

00:36:42.374 --> 00:36:43.971
- That was another thing I had to learn

00:36:43.971 --> 00:36:46.030
as well along the way.

00:36:46.030 --> 00:36:48.152
- When you started off,
did you get a lot of advice

00:36:48.152 --> 00:36:50.387
from, maybe, your fellow gem mates?

00:36:50.387 --> 00:36:53.449
Or over itself, about what to do in terms

00:36:53.449 --> 00:36:57.414
of how to handle your content
and just general advice

00:36:57.414 --> 00:36:58.793
to be a content creator in general

00:36:58.793 --> 00:37:01.293
was it all just trial by fire?

00:37:04.171 --> 00:37:06.263
- I'm going to be honest with you.

00:37:06.263 --> 00:37:08.843
We really were just
kinda, they just said go.

00:37:08.843 --> 00:37:10.602
And I think that part of that is,

00:37:10.602 --> 00:37:12.686
because in the process of selecting us,

00:37:12.686 --> 00:37:15.235
they've made sure that we
would be okay with that

00:37:15.235 --> 00:37:17.231
because all of us that
have gone through it,

00:37:17.231 --> 00:37:20.175
have not, there hasn't
been like a huge disaster

00:37:20.175 --> 00:37:21.817
or anything like that with
us in terms of streaming.

00:37:21.817 --> 00:37:23.933
Everyone has a good handle on themselves.

00:37:23.933 --> 00:37:27.518
And I think you got to
give it to, I guess,

00:37:27.518 --> 00:37:30.655
who chose us for that, specifically.

00:37:30.655 --> 00:37:33.636
They did choose people that have a handle

00:37:33.636 --> 00:37:34.658
on how to do things.

00:37:34.658 --> 00:37:35.491
However, that isn't to say

00:37:35.491 --> 00:37:37.977
that we were all experienced
streamers in the beginning.

00:37:37.977 --> 00:37:39.545
'Cause that's definitely not the case,

00:37:39.545 --> 00:37:41.072
I had barely streamed in my entire life,

00:37:41.072 --> 00:37:43.397
I was mostly making music in the shadows.

00:37:43.397 --> 00:37:44.361
- [Connor] Quite different.

00:37:44.361 --> 00:37:45.765
- Extremely different.

00:37:45.765 --> 00:37:48.787
But somehow they knew that I'd be able

00:37:48.787 --> 00:37:50.245
to handle it, somehow.

00:37:50.245 --> 00:37:51.743
And you learn a lot of
things along the way,

00:37:51.743 --> 00:37:53.826
but in the beginning we
all did have the ability

00:37:53.826 --> 00:37:55.648
to get through that starting line,

00:37:55.648 --> 00:37:57.609
but we didn't get a lot of advice,

00:37:57.609 --> 00:37:58.644
I will be honest about that.

00:37:58.644 --> 00:38:00.087
- I think one of the biggest
things you have to learn,

00:38:00.087 --> 00:38:02.509
which a lot of people,
maybe, might not consider,

00:38:02.509 --> 00:38:04.026
is the technical side of things

00:38:04.026 --> 00:38:06.933
because with a YouTube video,

00:38:06.933 --> 00:38:09.186
if something technical
happens on set or something,

00:38:09.186 --> 00:38:11.612
some things you could
kind of save and post

00:38:11.612 --> 00:38:14.108
there is no getting around
technical issues on stream.

00:38:14.108 --> 00:38:15.011
- Exactly.

00:38:15.011 --> 00:38:16.691
- And I feel like the only
way you've really learned

00:38:16.691 --> 00:38:18.630
is when it happens on
stream and you're just like,

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:20.720
"well, I guess I just have to learn not

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:21.777
to do that again, right?"

00:38:21.777 --> 00:38:25.421
- Stuff breaks a lot as
well, it's normal, right?

00:38:25.421 --> 00:38:29.173
Yeah, damn, so you mainly
did a lot of it on your own,

00:38:29.173 --> 00:38:31.886
you learned everything, wow.

00:38:31.886 --> 00:38:34.021
So when you started off,
there was nobody who was like,

00:38:34.021 --> 00:38:37.297
"Hey, by the way, people might
just say bad things about you

00:38:37.297 --> 00:38:38.725
for no reason, that might just happen,

00:38:38.725 --> 00:38:40.019
just like, it's gonna happen."

00:38:40.019 --> 00:38:40.852
No one told you?

00:38:40.852 --> 00:38:43.462
- There was a question that I will say,

00:38:43.462 --> 00:38:47.427
because it's kind of obvious
that they would ask us this.

00:38:47.427 --> 00:38:50.323
People are going to say mean
things about you and hate you,

00:38:50.323 --> 00:38:51.949
are you okay with that?

00:38:51.949 --> 00:38:54.108
And obviously all of us got in.

00:38:54.108 --> 00:38:55.413
(Garnt laughs)

00:38:55.413 --> 00:38:59.291
We all said yes, but I recall
that my answer, specifically,

00:38:59.291 --> 00:39:00.794
was, well, I've dealt with that,

00:39:00.794 --> 00:39:04.330
with my music before all of this.

00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:06.806
I did indeed have people
that would come around

00:39:06.806 --> 00:39:09.357
and do a little bit of
trolling, so to speak.

00:39:09.357 --> 00:39:12.718
And I felt like I had
dealt with them pretty well

00:39:12.718 --> 00:39:13.757
by just not saying anything.

00:39:13.757 --> 00:39:14.941
- [Connor] 100%.
- And I feel like,

00:39:14.941 --> 00:39:16.827
I thought to myself, that I should be fine

00:39:16.827 --> 00:39:18.706
when I enter holo and it's been fine.

00:39:18.706 --> 00:39:20.796
So that's okay for me.

00:39:20.796 --> 00:39:23.338
Everyone's experience though is different,

00:39:23.338 --> 00:39:24.383
I would say, so.

00:39:24.383 --> 00:39:28.296
- Yeah, I think as well,
there is the added,

00:39:28.296 --> 00:39:30.611
very scary aspect of it being,

00:39:30.611 --> 00:39:33.648
again, this is just my
perception of being a VTuber,

00:39:33.648 --> 00:39:35.053
everyone is just trying to find out

00:39:35.053 --> 00:39:37.332
as much personal information
about you as possible.

00:39:37.332 --> 00:39:40.387
Whereas everyone can just
see that I'm a teenager now

00:39:40.387 --> 00:39:41.220
and I'm-

00:39:41.220 --> 00:39:42.466
(Garnt and Mori laughs)

00:39:42.466 --> 00:39:45.386
I feel like older people are definitely,

00:39:45.386 --> 00:39:47.267
big people, trying to get
our personal information,

00:39:47.267 --> 00:39:49.206
but I don't feel like it's intense.

00:39:49.206 --> 00:39:52.124
At least I don't see it,
maybe, I'm just too lazy,

00:39:52.124 --> 00:39:53.141
I'm not looking, but-

00:39:53.141 --> 00:39:54.418
- Or you've already shown everything

