good piece of advice,

00:47:14.220 --> 00:47:17.222
and I appreciate it as advice
and I appreciate it as advice.

00:47:17.222 --> 00:47:18.055
- That's a good piece of life advice.

00:47:18.055 --> 00:47:18.888
(Joey and Garnt giggle)

00:47:18.888 --> 00:47:20.043
- [Mori] When it's not in all caps.

00:47:20.043 --> 00:47:21.204
- The same thing applies in real life.

00:47:21.204 --> 00:47:24.173
(Garnt and Mori laughs)

00:47:24.173 --> 00:47:25.198
- That is true.

00:47:25.198 --> 00:47:27.374
I'm more likely to listen
to it if it's phrased,

00:47:27.374 --> 00:47:28.410
not in all caps though.

00:47:28.410 --> 00:47:31.612
That's a general, just a good
idea for chat, I would say.

00:47:31.612 --> 00:47:33.594
In general, I don't really
actually care too much

00:47:33.594 --> 00:47:34.752
about back seating.

00:47:34.752 --> 00:47:39.292
It's when it gets to be
everyone and super aggressive

00:47:39.292 --> 00:47:40.511
that my energy begins to fall.

00:47:40.511 --> 00:47:43.611
So I had to kind of learn to
like, "Hey Calli, get over it."

00:47:43.611 --> 00:47:46.028
Just try and turn your vision

00:47:47.796 --> 00:47:49.909
into a way that it can
just pick out comments,

00:47:49.909 --> 00:47:52.826
and chat that aren't entirely aggressive,

00:47:52.826 --> 00:47:54.373
and you keep the stream going.

00:47:54.373 --> 00:47:56.783
And so I do like interacting
with chat these days.

00:47:56.783 --> 00:47:59.981
Chat can be funny, they have some jokes,

00:47:59.981 --> 00:48:02.506
you guys have jokes out there, I will say.

00:48:02.506 --> 00:48:04.671
And they really do kind
of brighten up my day,

00:48:04.671 --> 00:48:06.754
so it's been worth it in the end,

00:48:06.754 --> 00:48:09.251
but I'd say that my favorite
kinds of streams to do,

00:48:09.251 --> 00:48:11.530
probably variety streams.

00:48:11.530 --> 00:48:14.030
Though they do take the most preparation.

00:48:14.030 --> 00:48:15.845
Recently, I've been trying
to do a Kiowa stream.

00:48:15.845 --> 00:48:19.568
So English learning by speaking
simple and slow English

00:48:19.568 --> 00:48:21.629
for anyone really not
only Japanese viewers,

00:48:21.629 --> 00:48:23.242
but people around the world
who maybe have English

00:48:23.242 --> 00:48:24.483
as a second language.

00:48:24.483 --> 00:48:26.501
That takes a lot of preparation.

00:48:26.501 --> 00:48:27.462
- Oh, when you said English learning,

00:48:27.462 --> 00:48:29.128
I was like you learning English?

00:48:29.128 --> 00:48:30.081
(Mori and Garnt laughs)

00:48:30.081 --> 00:48:31.184
I think you've already got that down.

00:48:31.184 --> 00:48:34.079
(Garnt laughs)

00:48:34.079 --> 00:48:35.820
Guys, I'm going to do an
English learning stream today.

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:37.283
- Yeah, gonna do my best.

00:48:37.283 --> 00:48:38.116
- English learning stream,

00:48:38.116 --> 00:48:40.423
next week we're gonna perform the PokeRap.

00:48:40.423 --> 00:48:43.341
- To be fair, you should do
a Duolingo English speed run.

00:48:43.341 --> 00:48:45.741
See how good you are at
English, see if you could do it.

00:48:45.741 --> 00:48:46.759
- [Mori] That's a good idea.

00:48:46.759 --> 00:48:48.347
- I feel like I would be
way worse than I think

00:48:48.347 --> 00:48:50.390
I have any right to be
at English in Duolingo.

00:48:50.390 --> 00:48:52.590
- I feel like, I don't know,

00:48:52.590 --> 00:48:53.987
that's an interesting stream actually.

00:48:53.987 --> 00:48:56.108
Seeing if you are as fluent in English,

00:48:56.108 --> 00:48:59.992
as something like Duolingo thinks you are.

00:48:59.992 --> 00:49:01.211
- What's the main language then?

00:49:01.211 --> 00:49:02.991
That you have the application set on

00:49:02.991 --> 00:49:04.090
if you're learning English?

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:05.530
Can it be English again?

00:49:05.530 --> 00:49:06.546
(Garnt chuckles)

00:49:06.546 --> 00:49:07.379
- [Connor] I guess so.

00:49:07.379 --> 00:49:08.589
- That's a good question.

00:49:08.589 --> 00:49:11.565
(Garnt, Mori and Joey laughs)

00:49:11.565 --> 00:49:12.577
- [Joey] I can't it explain it too then.

00:49:12.577 --> 00:49:14.601
- I don't think Duolingo has
ever thought about that before.

00:49:14.601 --> 00:49:15.962
- Yeah, I guess so.

00:49:15.962 --> 00:49:16.795
- This is gonna so bad.

00:49:16.795 --> 00:49:18.281
So what do I'm learning Japanese, right?

00:49:18.281 --> 00:49:21.921
And they're like, oh
yeah, it's like a pronoun,

00:49:21.921 --> 00:49:24.922
and I was like, yeah, for
the first time I hear it.

00:49:24.922 --> 00:49:25.755
I was like, what is that again?

00:49:25.755 --> 00:49:26.721
(Joey laughs)

00:49:26.721 --> 00:49:27.554
I was like-

00:49:27.554 --> 00:49:28.387
- What's a pronoun?

00:49:28.387 --> 00:49:30.100
- No, I know but you
know when you hear it,

00:49:30.100 --> 00:49:31.493
you're like, I haven't heard that in,

00:49:31.493 --> 00:49:32.575
when you got the different types

00:49:32.575 --> 00:49:33.692
of verbs and nouns, you're
like, wait, hold on,

00:49:33.692 --> 00:49:35.852
let me refresh my memory of
what that is again, hold up.

00:49:35.852 --> 00:49:36.878
It's been like five years, you know.

00:49:36.878 --> 00:49:38.189
- I'm trying to remember
what a pronoun is.

00:49:38.189 --> 00:49:40.240
(Joey laughs)

00:49:40.240 --> 00:49:42.718
- 'Cause they obviously think
when the Japanese teacher

00:49:42.718 --> 00:49:43.551
is teaching me Japanese, they're like,

00:49:43.551 --> 00:49:45.325
oh, you know this stuff, it's English.

00:49:45.325 --> 00:49:46.666
It's fluent, you speak it, right?

00:49:46.666 --> 00:49:48.546
And it's like, yeah, yeah,
I remember that stuff,

00:49:48.546 --> 00:49:49.603
I remember fluently.

00:49:49.603 --> 00:49:51.343
- I don't think that works when it comes

00:49:51.343 --> 00:49:53.469
to learning languages
though, to me at least.

00:49:53.469 --> 00:49:54.506
When I hear something is a pronoun,

00:49:54.506 --> 00:49:56.209
I don't think I remember what,

00:49:56.209 --> 00:49:57.486
well, as we've just established,

00:49:57.486 --> 00:50:00.644
we don't know what a fucking
pronoun is in our own language.

00:50:00.644 --> 00:50:01.664
(Mori chuckles)

00:50:01.664 --> 00:50:02.803
How the fuck are we going to
learn that in another language?

00:50:02.803 --> 00:50:04.274
- That's why it's so
embarrassing when I'm there

00:50:04.274 --> 00:50:05.944
in front of the teacher who is Japanese,

00:50:05.944 --> 00:50:07.500
and she's like, oh, it's
just like this thing,

00:50:07.500 --> 00:50:08.545
and I'm like, yeah.

00:50:08.545 --> 00:50:09.556
(Garnt laughs)

00:50:09.556 --> 00:50:10.583
Yeah.

00:50:10.583 --> 00:50:11.416
- [Mori] I know what that is.

00:50:11.416 --> 00:50:12.249
- Yeah, exactly, right?

00:50:12.249 --> 00:50:13.200
I'm like, totally, I know what that is.

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:14.259
(Joey laughs)

00:50:14.259 --> 00:50:15.092
Yeah, okay.

00:50:15.092 --> 00:50:15.925
- I know what an adjective is.

00:50:15.925 --> 00:50:17.002
(Joey and Connor laughs)

00:50:17.002 --> 00:50:18.976
- Adjective and noun
and verbs are the ones

00:50:18.976 --> 00:50:20.979
that you should know and then
anything else, I'm like, yeah.

00:50:20.979 --> 00:50:21.979
- A pronoun.

00:50:23.356 --> 00:50:24.421
- I know pronouns, yeah.

00:50:24.421 --> 00:50:25.460
- Proverbs.

00:50:25.460 --> 00:50:26.581
- Fuck but I don't know what that is.

00:50:26.581 --> 00:50:28.902
I mean, I know that it exists
but if you asked me right now.

00:50:28.902 --> 00:50:30.603
- I know it exists but
I don't know what it is.

00:50:30.603 --> 00:50:31.462
- This is bad, right?

00:50:31.462 --> 00:50:32.925
I should know this, this is my language.

00:50:32.925 --> 00:50:36.443
- Tell me how to use a semi-colon.

00:50:36.443 --> 00:50:37.441
(Garnt laughs)

00:50:37.441 --> 00:50:38.599
(Mori laughs)

00:50:38.599 --> 00:50:42.532
I don't know if you just heard
the groan across this office

00:50:42.532 --> 00:50:43.365
that we just had.

00:50:43.365 --> 00:50:44.198
- I will use a semi-colon,

00:50:44.198 --> 00:50:46.131
I don't think I'm remotely correct.

00:50:46.131 --> 00:50:48.215
But I will throw one in
there just for good measure.

00:50:48.215 --> 00:50:51.321
- I kind of use it like
using a comma and a full stop

00:50:51.321 --> 00:50:52.958
at the same time, kind of sentence.

00:50:52.958 --> 00:50:54.635
- You actually know the way that I think

00:50:54.635 --> 00:50:56.777
I learned how to use a semi-colon?

00:50:56.777 --> 00:50:58.403
It's the Lonely Island song, Semi Colon.

00:50:58.403 --> 00:50:59.236
(Joey laughs)

00:50:59.236 --> 00:51:00.602
It's a great example.

00:51:00.602 --> 00:51:01.635
- Oh that's right, I remember that.

00:51:01.635 --> 00:51:05.027
- I was like, oh, that's
the first time I've seen

00:51:05.027 --> 00:51:07.023
the explanation of
semi-colon where I'm like,

00:51:07.023 --> 00:51:09.068
that makes sense now, that makes sense.

00:51:09.068 --> 00:51:12.429
- I dunno, I just feel like
we're the era of auto-corrects,

00:51:12.429 --> 00:51:13.450
and shit like that.

00:51:13.450 --> 00:51:15.697
We just pay way less attention to it.

00:51:15.697 --> 00:51:17.915
I've just started using
apostrophes in places

00:51:17.915 --> 00:51:19.911
where apostrophes don't need to be used,

00:51:19.911 --> 00:51:21.154
but it just looks right.

00:51:21.154 --> 00:51:22.791
And I know it's wrong sometimes.

00:51:22.791 --> 00:51:25.781
- Well, it's like with
languages like Japanese,

00:51:25.781 --> 00:51:28.253
sometimes, some people, some
of the younger generation

00:51:28.253 --> 00:51:30.012
don't know how to write the Kanji,

00:51:30.012 --> 00:51:31.092
'cause they're typing it all the time.

00:51:31.092 --> 00:51:33.326
And that's a thing.

00:51:33.326 --> 00:51:34.446
- Yeah.

00:51:34.446 --> 00:51:35.503
- Is that true, Joey?

00:51:35.503 --> 00:51:36.542
- Yes.

00:51:36.542 --> 00:51:37.375
(Garnt laughs)

00:51:37.375 --> 00:51:38.208
Absolutely, I mean-

00:51:38.208 --> 00:51:39.295
- Especially in other countries
with characters as well,

00:51:39.295 --> 00:51:40.128
that's a big problem.

00:51:40.128 --> 00:51:42.213
- Yeah, yeah 'cause when I was in school,

00:51:42.213 --> 00:51:44.396
I took the Kanji proficiency test,

00:51:44.396 --> 00:51:47.228
so obviously I was just
used to writing it all.

00:51:47.228 --> 00:51:49.568
But once I got that degree,
I was like, "All right, cool,

00:51:49.568 --> 00:51:51.188
I never have to write
this shit ever again."

00:51:51.188 --> 00:51:52.888
And now when I look at it,

00:51:52.888 --> 00:51:54.874
or now when someone's like,
"How do you write this?"

00:51:54.874 --> 00:51:56.633
when someone's like,
"How do you write this?"

00:51:56.633 --> 00:51:58.328
I just kind of stop and I'm
like, "I should know this."

00:51:58.328 --> 00:51:59.489
- Well you can read it, right?

00:51:59.489 --> 00:52:01.046
So it doesn't matter.
- I can read it,

00:52:01.046 --> 00:52:03.137
but I can't write it and there's
so many symbols like that.

00:52:03.137 --> 00:52:05.363
- Yeah, you've done language
learning streams as well.

00:52:05.363 --> 00:52:06.947
Japanese as well, isn't it?

00:52:06.947 --> 00:52:08.103
How's that been going?

00:52:08.103 --> 00:52:09.903
- I can't really do them as much anymore

00:52:09.903 --> 00:52:11.980
because of certain permissions issues.

00:52:11.980 --> 00:52:15.380
But I'm thinking of getting
back into it with just,

00:52:15.380 --> 00:52:17.066
making a list of vocabulary beforehand,

00:52:17.066 --> 00:52:20.004
and running through flashcards with chat.

00:52:20.004 --> 00:52:22.564
Because I feel like
that can be entertaining

00:52:22.564 --> 00:52:23.562
in its own way, I think.

00:52:23.562 --> 00:52:25.467
Somehow it always becomes a fun time,

00:52:25.467 --> 00:52:27.562
even ideas that I think are
going to be kind of boring,

00:52:27.562 --> 00:52:28.885
it ends up being fun.

00:52:28.885 --> 00:52:31.159
- You know what's amazed
me about streaming?

00:52:31.159 --> 00:52:33.158
No matter what obscure thing I do,

00:52:33.158 --> 00:52:36.116
just one God damn expert appears
out of nowhere in the chat.

00:52:36.116 --> 00:52:37.143
(Joey laughs)

00:52:37.143 --> 00:52:42.068
I'll be doing Sudoku and the
2017 world Sudoku champion

00:52:42.068 --> 00:52:43.803
will come on my chat and be like,

00:52:43.803 --> 00:52:45.563
"Hey, what are you doing?
This technique's cringe."

00:52:45.563 --> 00:52:46.396
(Joey laughs)

00:52:46.396 --> 00:52:47.229
You know what I mean?

00:52:47.229 --> 00:52:48.062
No matter what I do, somehow,

00:52:48.062 --> 00:52:48.901
an expert comes out of nowhere.

00:52:48.901 --> 00:52:50.201
- What is this, fucking Reddit?

00:52:50.201 --> 00:52:51.034
(Garnt and Mori laughs)

00:52:51.034 --> 00:52:54.101
- It's insane, I'd be doing
a water grading stream,

00:52:54.101 --> 00:52:57.037
and the guy who designed
water will appear in my chat.

00:52:57.037 --> 00:52:58.474
(Garnt chuckles)

00:52:58.474 --> 00:52:59.331
- "Hi, I discovered water."

00:52:59.331 --> 00:53:00.463
- [Garnt] "I discovered water."

00:53:00.463 --> 00:53:01.514
(Garnt laughs)

00:53:01.514 --> 00:53:02.419
- It's insane, I don't
know how this happens,

00:53:02.419 --> 00:53:03.435
no matter what I do.

00:53:03.435 --> 00:53:04.468
- Connor, when he sees a Badei stream.

00:53:04.468 --> 00:53:05.496
(Mori and Garnt laughs)

00:53:05.496 --> 00:53:09.756
- A Toto CEO comes into my
chat, you're using it wrong.

00:53:09.756 --> 00:53:11.193
- Kind of in the same vein,

00:53:11.193 --> 00:53:14.296
sometimes there'll be a movie or a song

00:53:14.296 --> 00:53:17.314
that I know for a fact only
10,000 people have heard

00:53:17.314 --> 00:53:20.228
or listened to and someone
in chat knows about it.

00:53:20.228 --> 00:53:21.250
Oh, I remember that.

00:53:21.250 --> 00:53:22.370
Wait, do you?

00:53:22.370 --> 00:53:23.389
And they do.

00:53:23.389 --> 00:53:24.222
- Yeah, I'm the producer.

00:53:24.222 --> 00:53:25.055
(everyone laughs)

00:53:25.055 --> 00:53:25.888
- I made that.
- Of course.

00:53:25.888 --> 00:53:27.448
- Or like that very
obscure childhood memory

00:53:27.448 --> 00:53:30.364
that you have of a game or a
show and then you describe it,

00:53:30.364 --> 00:53:32.443
and then chat is like, oh
yeah, I know what that is,

00:53:32.443 --> 00:53:33.276
of course, I know what that is.

00:53:33.276 --> 00:53:34.109
(Joey snickering)
I'm like, what?

00:53:34.109 --> 00:53:34.942
- [Mori] Wild.

00:53:34.942 --> 00:53:35.775
- How do you know that?

00:53:35.775 --> 00:53:36.983
- Always be wild to me.
- What's wrong with you guys?

00:53:36.983 --> 00:53:39.685
- They just know it, man.
- Chat is a curse,

00:53:39.685 --> 00:53:42.042
but also like an almighty being
that just knows everything.

00:53:42.042 --> 00:53:43.232
It's pretty impressive.

00:53:43.232 --> 00:53:44.074
- It is.

00:53:44.074 --> 00:53:45.569
- Or it's like a conglomerate, right?

00:53:45.569 --> 00:53:49.153
It's weird, seeing people refer to chat,

00:53:49.153 --> 00:53:52.236
it's like, yes, it's, I don't know,

00:53:52.236 --> 00:53:53.069
such a weird concept to me.

00:53:53.069 --> 00:53:55.551
'Cause everyone in chat
is a individual person,

00:53:55.551 --> 00:53:59.384
but then chat just melds
into this one entity.

00:54:00.647 --> 00:54:01.700
- An entity, yeah.

00:54:01.700 --> 00:54:04.820
- One all-knowing entity
that is a equally-

00:54:04.820 --> 00:54:06.196
- All single brain cell
that's joined together.

00:54:06.196 --> 00:54:07.247
(Mori and Garnt laughs)

00:54:07.247 --> 00:54:08.111
- Omnipotent, omnipresent being.

00:54:08.111 --> 00:54:10.361
- [Mori] To make one brain.

00:54:11.272 --> 00:54:12.454
- You get a 300 IQ.

00:54:12.454 --> 00:54:14.903
Oh my God.
- [Mori] Yeah, that's right,

00:54:14.903 --> 00:54:16.046
that's true.

00:54:16.046 --> 00:54:17.040
- It's like being part of hololive,

00:54:17.040 --> 00:54:19.502
of course you stream on YouTube,

00:54:19.502 --> 00:54:21.312
but what do you think about

00:54:21.312 --> 00:54:22.845
the whole Twitch streaming as well?

00:54:22.845 --> 00:54:25.167
Have you ever been interested
in streaming on Twitch,

00:54:25.167 --> 00:54:27.721
and what kind of differences
do you think that would make

00:54:27.721 --> 00:54:29.009
if you ever did?

00:54:29.009 --> 00:54:31.408
- It's pretty unexplored
territory to be honest,

00:54:31.408 --> 00:54:33.194
so I haven't really
thought too much about it.

00:54:33.194 --> 00:54:34.268
I feel like,

00:54:34.268 --> 00:54:36.473
I have watched a couple
streamers on Twitch,

00:54:36.473 --> 00:54:38.313
but for whatever reason,

00:54:38.313 --> 00:54:40.747
I just feel like I gravitate
more towards YouTube.

00:54:40.747 --> 00:54:42.963
I know that they both have
their pros and cons, for sure.

00:54:42.963 --> 00:54:44.768
I've gotten so comfortable on YouTube.

00:54:44.768 --> 00:54:46.144
And one thing that I really love about it,

00:54:46.144 --> 00:54:49.083
on YouTube especially, is that archives

00:54:49.083 --> 00:54:50.085
are kept forever.

00:54:50.085 --> 00:54:53.541
So my streams become videos
that people can just watch

00:54:53.541 --> 00:54:56.488
whenever they want and
check out the whole backlog.

00:54:56.488 --> 00:54:57.487
I really love that,

00:54:57.487 --> 00:54:59.588
but I do think that, for example,

00:54:59.588 --> 00:55:02.989
Twitch's sub feature is superior.

00:55:02.989 --> 00:55:04.708
That is for certain.

00:55:04.708 --> 00:55:06.593
But I think they both
have their pros and cons,

00:55:06.593 --> 00:55:09.014
I just feel at this point
it's like a comfort thing.

00:55:09.014 --> 00:55:11.701
I'm just so used to YouTube
now and the way that people,

00:55:11.701 --> 00:55:13.379
interact with each other in chat there.

00:55:13.379 --> 00:55:15.735
There's so many layers to it
that I just kind of prefer.

00:55:15.735 --> 00:55:18.296
- Yeah, I find it really
curious or really weird

00:55:18.296 --> 00:55:19.956
to see both these platforms,

00:55:19.956 --> 00:55:21.999
just having different
cultures around them.

00:55:21.999 --> 00:55:23.648
And it's almost like
a different etiquette.

00:55:23.648 --> 00:55:25.053
Like, when you're on a Twitch stream,

00:55:25.053 --> 00:55:28.772
it feels completely different
to watching a YouTube stream.

00:55:28.772 --> 00:55:32.414
And I'm not sure if
it's just the community

00:55:32.414 --> 00:55:33.993
or if it's just emotes or what it is.

00:55:33.993 --> 00:55:38.961
But yeah, both platforms
to me feel oddly different

00:55:38.961 --> 00:55:40.999
whenever I'm watching a
different, greater stream

00:55:40.999 --> 00:55:42.036
on that platform.

00:55:42.036 --> 00:55:44.412
- There's a culture to it,
for sure, I have noticed

00:55:44.412 --> 00:55:47.414
'cause I watched streamers
who stream on both,

00:55:47.414 --> 00:55:49.672
and it is quite different, I got to say.

00:55:49.672 --> 00:55:50.731
(Mori chuckles)

00:55:50.731 --> 00:55:51.616
(Garnt laughs)

00:55:51.616 --> 00:55:53.602
That's not me being, I'm not
throwing any shade at Twitch,

00:55:53.602 --> 00:55:55.639
I'm just saying that-

00:55:55.639 --> 00:55:56.472
- Throw shade at them.

00:55:56.472 --> 00:55:57.978
(Garnt laughs)

00:55:57.978 --> 00:55:58.811
Do it.

00:55:58.811 --> 00:55:59.644
- [Mori] I mean, it's not for me.

00:55:59.644 --> 00:56:00.477
- They're not paying you.

00:56:00.477 --> 00:56:02.959
- [Mori] It's not for me,
but I mean, at the same time,

00:56:02.959 --> 00:56:03.792
it's just what you prefer.

00:56:03.792 --> 00:56:05.298
At the end of the day,
everyone's got their preference,

00:56:05.298 --> 00:56:07.276
and just choose what you
like and that's how it goes.

00:56:07.276 --> 00:56:09.414
- Like yeah, I don't think I'd have

00:56:09.414 --> 00:56:11.591
a difficult time streaming on YouTube now

00:56:11.591 --> 00:56:13.175
that I'm just so used to it.
- You're used to it, yeah.

00:56:13.175 --> 00:56:14.296
You get used to it.

00:56:14.296 --> 00:56:15.129
- Yeah and vice versa.

00:56:15.129 --> 00:56:15.962
- The vibes different as well, I think.

00:56:15.962 --> 00:56:16.795
- [Mori] Yeah, exactly.

00:56:16.795 --> 00:56:18.846
- The vibe definitely
is totally different.

00:56:18.846 --> 00:56:21.092
People are way more
joking on Twitch, I think.

00:56:21.092 --> 00:56:22.091
People are used to-

00:56:22.091 --> 00:56:22.924
- People are monkeys on Twitch.

00:56:22.924 --> 00:56:23.757
(Garnt and Mori laughs)

00:56:23.757 --> 00:56:24.590
- [Joey] Yeah, they are.

00:56:24.590 --> 00:56:25.928
- We got a couple monkeys
on YouTube as well though.

00:56:25.928 --> 00:56:26.761
- Oh that's true.

00:56:26.761 --> 00:56:28.686
- But it is a, kind of
different feeling for sure.

00:56:28.686 --> 00:56:30.098
At the end of the day though,

00:56:30.098 --> 00:56:31.989
I will go to where the creator I like is,

00:56:31.989 --> 00:56:34.101
that is the truth for me personally,

00:56:34.101 --> 00:56:35.988
but that's not always
the case for everyone

00:56:35.988 --> 00:56:37.943
everyone's kind of different.

00:56:37.943 --> 00:56:40.081
- Some people are lazy to open
another tab, I don't get it.

00:56:40.081 --> 00:56:42.444
- I get it, I mean-

00:56:42.444 --> 00:56:45.293
(Chad, Mori, Joey and Connor laughs)

00:56:45.293 --> 00:56:46.308
- Garnt is that guy.

00:56:46.308 --> 00:56:49.020
- Clicking a link to go on
another platform, get outta here.

00:56:49.020 --> 00:56:50.862
(Mori and Joey laughs)

00:56:50.862 --> 00:56:51.695
No, I mean-
- Too much time.

00:56:51.695 --> 00:56:53.986
- My poor fingy, can't handle it.

00:56:53.986 --> 00:56:55.045
- No because, I mean,

00:56:55.045 --> 00:56:57.250
there's just so many platforms nowadays,

00:56:57.250 --> 00:56:59.985
and so many different apps and stuff

00:56:59.985 --> 00:57:01.765
that sometimes you just-

00:57:01.765 --> 00:57:02.598
- sounds like my mum.

00:57:02.598 --> 00:57:04.165
"There's too many apps these days."

00:57:04.165 --> 00:57:05.488
- I can't handle all the tabs.

00:57:05.488 --> 00:57:10.387
- Sometimes you just build up
a, is schedule the right word?

00:57:10.387 --> 00:57:12.448
No, it's a pattern, you build up a pattern

00:57:12.448 --> 00:57:14.326
about what time you
spend on each app, right.

00:57:14.326 --> 00:57:17.382
So I've been watching a lot
of YouTube shorts nowadays,

00:57:17.382 --> 00:57:18.764
even though I could go,

00:57:18.764 --> 00:57:20.842
and even though they're just
recycled TikToks, right.

00:57:20.842 --> 00:57:24.023
But it's just because I'm, as a YouTuber,

00:57:24.023 --> 00:57:25.102
I'm just on YouTube more.

00:57:25.102 --> 00:57:26.722
That is just more convenient for me.

00:57:26.722 --> 00:57:29.979
And so I don't like, if you
watch a lot of Twitch streamers,

00:57:29.979 --> 00:57:32.098
then sure, it's easy to
watch more Twitch streamers.

00:57:32.098 --> 00:57:34.182
But if you're not like
integrated into that kind

00:57:34.182 --> 00:57:35.964
of ecosystem, it's weird.

00:57:35.964 --> 00:57:39.139
'Cause it feels like you're going more out

00:57:39.139 --> 00:57:42.065
of your way just to view
this one creator as opposed

00:57:42.065 --> 00:57:43.400
to just being on the platform naturally.

00:57:43.400 --> 00:57:47.446
So I do think it is a factor that matters,

00:57:47.446 --> 00:57:51.219
which is why you see a lot
of people who are just famous

00:57:51.219 --> 00:57:52.415
on one platform nowadays.

00:57:52.415 --> 00:57:53.895
You can have people who
are famous on YouTube

00:57:53.895 --> 00:57:56.792
and just not have a
presence anywhere else.

00:57:56.792 --> 00:57:57.814
- Right.

00:57:57.814 --> 00:57:58.812
- You know and I feel like

00:57:58.812 --> 00:58:01.970
the super, super
successful content creators

00:58:01.970 --> 00:58:03.569
are people who can break out of that,

00:58:03.569 --> 00:58:06.678
just one platform and be
able to have a presence

00:58:06.678 --> 00:58:07.694
on multiple platforms.

00:58:07.694 --> 00:58:09.576
- I mean, I am a person who kind of feels

00:58:09.576 --> 00:58:11.362
like consistencies is a big key,

00:58:11.362 --> 00:58:13.637
but some people are so big
that it doesn't matter.

00:58:13.637 --> 00:58:15.402
it doesn't matter where you go,

00:58:15.402 --> 00:58:17.120
people will follow kind of thing.

00:58:17.120 --> 00:58:21.683
But I do kind of see the
merit in having all your stuff

00:58:21.683 --> 00:58:22.803
in one place.

00:58:22.803 --> 00:58:24.295
It's also just personally what I prefer

00:58:24.295 --> 00:58:26.037
because of how my brain works.

00:58:26.037 --> 00:58:27.523
I like to have everything in one place,

00:58:27.523 --> 00:58:29.102
'cause I'm already a
really big scatterbrain

00:58:29.102 --> 00:58:30.118
in the first place.

00:58:30.118 --> 00:58:32.381
If I had stuff all over the
place, I'd lose my mind.

00:58:32.381 --> 00:58:33.624
So I kind of have to-

00:58:33.624 --> 00:58:35.388
- You're juggling enough
things as it is, honestly.

00:58:35.388 --> 00:58:36.221
(Garnt chuckles)

00:58:36.221 --> 00:58:37.368
- Yeah, there's a lot going on, so.

00:58:37.368 --> 00:58:40.244
- I'm just scared to have
everything on one platform.

00:58:40.244 --> 00:58:42.110
I'm that mentality of,

00:58:42.110 --> 00:58:44.577
don't put all your eggs in
one basket, kind of thing.

00:58:44.577 --> 00:58:45.410
- [Mori] Oh I see.

00:58:45.410 --> 00:58:46.458
- Yeah, I do think that,

00:58:46.458 --> 00:58:47.549
however music is the one thing

00:58:47.549 --> 00:58:50.016
that is easily able to hold its own.

00:58:50.016 --> 00:58:53.360
I mean, that's why every
YouTuber on earth tries

00:58:53.360 --> 00:58:55.078
to become a popstar or rapper.

00:58:55.078 --> 00:58:56.938
- To varying levels of success.

00:58:56.938 --> 00:58:59.039
- 'Cause that's where, money is good,

00:58:59.039 --> 00:59:02.342
longevity is good, you can
have time to work on it.

00:59:02.342 --> 00:59:03.381
Music is definitely-

00:59:03.381 --> 00:59:05.570
- Also, it doesn't matter
how old you get, right.

00:59:05.570 --> 00:59:06.842
You can still keep making music.

00:59:06.842 --> 00:59:07.704
- Yeah, as long as it sounds good.

00:59:07.704 --> 00:59:08.537
(Garnt laughs)

00:59:08.537 --> 00:59:10.266
On YouTube, you turn 30, bye-bye.

00:59:10.266 --> 00:59:12.266
Sorry Garnt, you're out.

00:59:13.288 --> 00:59:15.955
(all laughing)

00:59:18.732 --> 00:59:19.925
God, why was his reaction so.

00:59:19.925 --> 00:59:22.010
See, I mean 30 years old,
man, it's what happens.

00:59:22.010 --> 00:59:24.494
- I'm having trouble hearing already, man.

00:59:24.494 --> 00:59:25.577
(Connor and Garnt laughs)

00:59:25.577 --> 00:59:27.777
- It's me, really just
trying to age gate Garnt.

00:59:27.777 --> 00:59:28.793
(Connor and Joey laughs)

00:59:28.793 --> 00:59:30.247
- [Mori] You should become a VTuber.

00:59:30.247 --> 00:59:31.733
- Go to, where do old people stream Garnt?

00:59:31.733 --> 00:59:32.929
(Connor and Joey chuckles)

00:59:32.929 --> 00:59:35.071
- I tried to say a platform, I was like,

00:59:35.071 --> 00:59:36.493
I probably shouldn't.

00:59:36.493 --> 00:59:40.203
- Justin.tv, that's where
I did my first stream.

00:59:40.203 --> 00:59:41.962
- I'm sorry, we are
joking, we are friends,

00:59:41.962 --> 00:59:42.795
we're allowed to make
these jokes, all right.

00:59:42.795 --> 00:59:43.628
(Garnt laughs)

00:59:43.628 --> 00:59:44.461
- It's like, you have to clarify

00:59:44.461 --> 00:59:45.294
that you're bantering these days.

00:59:45.294 --> 00:59:46.127
- [Connor] Yeah, I know, right?

00:59:46.127 --> 00:59:47.302
- Let's calm down a little bit, you know.

00:59:47.302 --> 00:59:48.135
- Oh God.

00:59:48.135 --> 00:59:50.738
- Is it the same on a
hololive with the friends?

00:59:50.738 --> 00:59:52.700
Do those people get upset
when you mock each other?

00:59:52.700 --> 00:59:55.800
- I will say we like
to banter quite a bit,

00:59:55.800 --> 00:59:58.900
but we got to remind y'all again

00:59:58.900 --> 01:00:00.305
and we really don't have to,

01:00:00.305 --> 01:00:02.923
but I'm going to anyway, we
are all really good friends.

01:00:02.923 --> 01:00:04.282
- Yeah, exactly, yeah.

01:00:04.282 --> 01:00:06.453
- And so at the same time,

01:00:06.453 --> 01:00:10.028
a lot of us have kind of, I
mean, I know I certainly came up

01:00:10.028 --> 01:00:11.573
in friend groups where we would,

01:00:11.573 --> 01:00:14.590
throw shit at each other
and it was all in good fun.

01:00:14.590 --> 01:00:16.629
We never really kind
of step over the line,

01:00:16.629 --> 01:00:18.867
it's all stuff that
we're comfortable with.

01:00:18.867 --> 01:00:20.308
And it's just good fun.

01:00:20.308 --> 01:00:21.730
No one hates anyone.

01:00:21.730 --> 01:00:23.767
Just, sometimes you gotta calm down,

01:00:23.767 --> 01:00:24.784
take a step back.

01:00:24.784 --> 01:00:25.905
We know each other pretty well,

01:00:25.905 --> 01:00:28.831
and it's just like that,
sometimes, we're having fun.

01:00:28.831 --> 01:00:30.352
- There's really just some people though

01:00:30.352 --> 01:00:32.703
that just don't get
that whole bantering or,

01:00:32.703 --> 01:00:34.572
when is it over the line?

01:00:34.572 --> 01:00:35.917
There are people who are

01:00:35.917 --> 01:00:37.281
overanalyze conversations on streams,

01:00:37.281 --> 01:00:39.678
and YouTube videos where
it's like, this is the frame,

01:00:39.678 --> 01:00:40.839
this is the very frame where

01:00:40.839 --> 01:00:42.651
their friendship started to crumble.

01:00:42.651 --> 01:00:44.730
Have you seen them streaming since then?

01:00:44.730 --> 01:00:45.771
(Mori laughs)

01:00:45.771 --> 01:00:46.604
- So dramatic.
- Don't think so,

01:00:46.604 --> 01:00:47.437
not friends anymore.

01:00:47.437 --> 01:00:48.528
It's like, no, dude, it's fine.

01:00:48.528 --> 01:00:53.111
- Yeah, I feel even more so
with the VTubing community.

01:00:55.075 --> 01:00:56.172
Do you ever feel like a lot of people

01:00:56.172 --> 01:01:00.671
really, really analyze
what your behaviors are?

01:01:00.671 --> 01:01:01.710
- Yeah.

01:01:01.710 --> 01:01:02.651
- And maybe a bit too much sometimes?

01:01:02.651 --> 01:01:05.016
- Yeah I'd say so, I've
seen several paragraphs

01:01:05.016 --> 01:01:06.038
analyzing my personality.

01:01:06.038 --> 01:01:07.599
From my deepest insecurities to things

01:01:07.599 --> 01:01:10.735
that everyone already knows, etcetera.

01:01:10.735 --> 01:01:13.062
I've seen plenty of things like that.

01:01:13.062 --> 01:01:15.718
And it's gonna happen, it's gonna happen.

01:01:15.718 --> 01:01:17.207
When so much of yourself is put out there,

01:01:17.207 --> 01:01:19.738
that's just how it's gonna be.

01:01:19.738 --> 01:01:24.724
But I will say it is the
truth, we really do only give

01:01:24.724 --> 01:01:27.110
a percentage of ourselves when we stream,

01:01:27.110 --> 01:01:29.809
even though it may be
so many hours in a day,

01:01:29.809 --> 01:01:33.373
there's still a whole side
where we go off and live lives.

01:01:33.373 --> 01:01:35.289
I'm still, occasionally reaping

01:01:35.289 --> 01:01:39.011
the occasional soul, don't
tell Death Sensei about that.

01:01:39.011 --> 01:01:40.879
But I'm doing other stuff as well.

01:01:40.879 --> 01:01:42.777
And it's important to remember that,

01:01:42.777 --> 01:01:44.536
it's important to take a deep breath

01:01:44.536 --> 01:01:46.492
and realize that we've all got lives.

01:01:46.492 --> 01:01:48.878
- So where does music
fit into your career?

01:01:48.878 --> 01:01:51.293
How do you find time for that?

01:01:51.293 --> 01:01:53.580
Where do you find time for that?

01:01:53.580 --> 01:01:56.017
- I think that the only
only thing I can say is,

01:01:56.017 --> 01:01:58.979
it's the one thing that I
love doing so completely

01:01:58.979 --> 01:02:01.898
and utterly, that time,
just isn't a factor to me,

01:02:01.898 --> 01:02:03.074
energy isn't a factor to me.

01:02:03.074 --> 01:02:04.882
All of that just disappears in me wanting

01:02:04.882 --> 01:02:07.878
to create music and I don't
compromise anything for it.

01:02:07.878 --> 01:02:09.602
- I believe that's called a passion.

01:02:09.602 --> 01:02:10.722
(Joey snickers)

01:02:10.722 --> 01:02:11.555
(Garnt laughs)

01:02:11.555 --> 01:02:12.612
- That sounds like a passion to me.

01:02:12.612 --> 01:02:13.680
- Yeah, I think that is the word.

01:02:13.680 --> 01:02:14.513
- Yeah, sounds like passion to me.

01:02:14.513 --> 01:02:16.559
- So no matter what, I will
find a way to fit it in.

01:02:16.559 --> 01:02:19.636
Even if other things have to
kind of, take a back seat.

01:02:19.636 --> 01:02:21.332
Even things like streaming, etcetera,

01:02:21.332 --> 01:02:23.254
which will not make everyone happy

01:02:23.254 --> 01:02:24.682
but you get over it, you get over it.

01:02:24.682 --> 01:02:27.114
At the end of the day it's what I love.

01:02:27.114 --> 01:02:28.682
- Yeah prioritise what
you want to do, right?

01:02:28.682 --> 01:02:29.744
- Exactly.

01:02:29.744 --> 01:02:30.577
- [Joey] Yeah, exactly.

01:02:30.577 --> 01:02:32.519
- Why else am I here, you know?

01:02:32.519 --> 01:02:33.352
- Just to suffer.

01:02:33.352 --> 01:02:34.353
- [Mori] Just to suffer.

01:02:34.353 --> 01:02:35.186
- I wanted to say it.

01:02:35.186 --> 01:02:36.117
- [Mori] There's no other reason.

01:02:36.117 --> 01:02:38.430
- That's what I thought of.

01:02:38.430 --> 01:02:40.467
- We all thought it, we all thought it.

01:02:40.467 --> 01:02:44.181
- You can't not say that line
and not make me think that.

01:02:44.181 --> 01:02:47.140
- Exactly, I mean, if that
was the case, then I'd quit.

01:02:47.140 --> 01:02:49.357
And I mean, if you want me to stay here,

01:02:49.357 --> 01:02:52.654
I'm going to do what I want
to do, that's all I can say.

